course: dictionary
lesson: 2158
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. The word corn is a good example. Read the words around it first. Ask yourself: what is this sentence about, a farm, a foot, or a history book?
explain: «Maize, or corn, is a grass too.» The word maize sits right next to corn, and it means the tall plant that grows seeds on a cob. So here corn means that plant. Always test your meaning by putting it into the sentence and checking that it still makes sense.
explain: Other clues help too. «Corn on the cob» and «popcorn» are about food from the plant. «He had a corn on his toe that hurt with every step» is about skin, not a plant. The same word can mean very different things, so never stop at the first meaning you think of.
fact: Corn can mean a tall plant with yellow seeds on a cob, also called maize or sweetcorn. This meaning is used in the USA, Canada and Australia.
fact: In the UK and Ireland, corn can mean any grain crop, such as wheat, barley or oats.
fact: A corn is a small, hard, painful patch of skin on the foot.
fact: «Some hunted bison, some farmed corn, beans and squash» shows that corn was a key food crop for Native American peoples.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence. If the sentence still makes sense, you have probably found the right meaning.
exercise: «Some hunted bison, some farmed corn, beans and squash.» What is corn in this sentence? | A kind of bison | A lump on a tree | A hard patch of skin on the foot | *A crop that farmers grow for food
exercise: «He had a corn on his toe that hurt with every step.» What is a corn here? | A tall plant with seeds on a cob | A grain crop like wheat | *A small, hard, painful patch of skin | A yellow grass
exercise: In the UK, what can corn mean? | A kind of popcorn | *Any grain crop, such as wheat, barley or oats | A painful patch on the foot | A cob of sweetcorn with yellow seeds
test: «Corn cobs nearly always have an even number of rows.» Why is this so? | Because the rows are sold separately | Because cobs are always picked in the rain | *Because the kernels grow in pairs | Because farmers count the rows by hand
test: «Some of these can be composted, but only in special hot composters.» What does this sentence tell you about plastics made from corn? | They are made from sugar cane only | Every one can be eaten | *Some can be composted, but only in special hot composters | They never break down at all
exam: In the sentence «We shared a bowl of popcorn at the cinema», what does corn mean? | A grain crop such as barley | A kind of bowl | A small hard patch of skin | *A tall plant with seeds on a cob, and the food made from it
